    {
      "sample_index": 0,
      "text": "Given two fair dice, the total number of possible outcomes is $6 \\times 6 = 36$.\n\nThe desired outcomes are numbers $ab$ that give results consistent with rules of divisibility by 3.\n\nSince each die has six possible results, we can count the acceptable results of each die below:\n\n- Obtaining the number $2$ or $5$ as $a$ since these render a number divisible by 3 when added to any possible number on $b$.\n- For the number on $b$, there are three possibilities for any given die.\n\nTherefore there are $2 \\times 3 = 6$ desired outcomes.\n\nOur probability is given by $\\dfrac{\\text{Number of favorable outcomes}}{\\text{Number of possible outcomes}}$, so the answer is:\n\n$$\\dfrac{6}{36} = \\boxed{\\dfrac{1}{6}}$$",
