    {
      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "The total number of ways to get a 2-digit number divisible by 3 is 15, calculated as follows:\n- 3: $(1 + 4, 3)$\n- 6: $(1 + 5, 3)$\n- 9: $(1 + 8, 3)$\n- 12: $(4 + 8, 3)$\n- 15: $(5 + 8, 3)$\n- 18: $(8 + 4, 8)$\n- 21: $(4 + 7, 4 + 7)$\n- 24: $(8 + 8, 4 + 4)$\n- 27: $(4 + 9)$\n- 30: $(4 + 10)$\n- 33: $(4 + 11)$\n- 36: $(8 + 12)$\n- 39: $(1 + 9, 1 + 9)$\n- 42: $(9 + 3, 9 + 3)$\n\nThe number of good outcomes divisible by 3 is the number of outcomes divisible by 3 divided by 2. For example, for the outcome $(1 + 5, 3)$ and $(1 + 8, 3)$, there are two outcomes. In the case of $(a + b = 3a \\Rightarrow a = 3 - \\mathrm{A}$, there are no attractive outcomes since \\\n$6 + 6 = 15$.\\\nHence, the probability is $\\frac{15}{2 \\times 36}=\\frac{5}{24}$.",
